  "markdown": "## TL;DR\n\nI audited the web UI for icon-only controls without accessible names, native `title` use, and inconsistent wording. The real defect was narrower than the task suggested: the global tooltip helper strips a control's `title` on first hover or focus, so any icon-only control named only by `title` lost its accessible name after the first interaction. I gave the four affected components their own `aria-label`, unified one tooltip phrase, added a template-wide guard test, and wrote the audit at `docs/qa/2026-11-labels-tooltips-audit-qa.md`. Review round 1 found two false negatives in the guard; round 2 fixed both. The scoped `REPOOS_CHECK_CHANGED=main repoos check` passed.\n\n## Work and rationale\n\nI started with a grep for `title=\"` and `:title=` across `src/ui-app/src`. That returned about 100 hits, but most are not native bubbles. `src/ui-app/src/lib/tooltip.ts` installs one delegated, body-level tooltip that restyles any `title` into a popup, so \"native title\" here mostly means \"styled tooltip fed by a title attribute\". Replacing all of them would be a large refactor, so I left them and recorded that in the audit doc.\n\nThe more useful question was whether each icon-only control has a name. A small Bun scanner in `/tmp` (not committed) parsed `<button>` and `<a>` blocks in every `.vue` file. It found no unnamed controls, but I checked it against a synthetic icon button first, because a zero from a regex can just mean the regex is wrong.\n\nA second scan, for icon-only controls whose only name was a `title`, found eight. The tooltip code explains why that matters: `enter()` calls `textFor()`, which removes `title` and stores it in `data-tip`, so the accessible name survives only until the first `mouseover` or `focusin`. Three of the eight were `FloatingHeads` head buttons whose `<img alt>` already names them. The other five were real:\n\n- `IntegrationStatusBar.vue`: the pipeline collapse toggle.\n- `AgentCard.vue`: the instructions expand/collapse toggle.\n- `AgentModelModal.vue`: two favorite star buttons, one per state.\n- `DiffView.vue`: the previous-file and next-file arrows.\n\nI added `aria-label` to each and kept `title`, so the styled tooltip still shows. I considered a central fix in `tooltip.ts` that copies `title` into `aria-label` for controls without visible text, and rejected it for this pass because it renames any control that goes through the helper. It is a listed follow-up.\n\nFor wording, the drawer's full-screen diff button said \"Open the full-screen diff view\" while the other diff controls say \"Open full-screen diff\". I unified the drawer to the shorter form and checked that no test asserted the old string. The removal and dismissal verbs (\"Remove attachment\", \"Delete schedule\", \"Dismiss this error\", \"Dismiss notification\") differ, but picking one verb is a product decision, so I documented them.\n\nThe regression test is `src/ui-app/tests/icon-control-names.test.ts`. Its main assertion is a template-wide ratchet that fails if any button or link under `src/ui-app/src` has no visible text and no name attribute (or non-empty `<img alt>`). It also pins the four new `aria-label` values and the shared diff wording. I wrote it as a source scan rather than a mounted-component test, because mounting `AgentModelModal` and `DiffView` needs store and route setup that would dwarf the check.\n\nReview round 1 found two false negatives. The open-tag regex stopped at the first `>`, so an attribute such as `:disabled=\"i > 0\"` (present in `LoginView.vue`) could make an unnamed control look like it had text. And a blanket `v-bind=` exemption skipped any bound control. In round 2 I made the open-tag match quote-aware and removed the exemption. The template-wide scan still finds nothing with the exemption gone, so no real control was hiding behind it. I added fixtures for each case: a `>` inside a quoted value, a `v-bind` control with no name, and an empty `alt`. All 15 test cases pass.\n\nThe round-1 review also asked for the macOS Hub to be covered. I verified by reading `macos/RepoOSHub/HubSidebarControls.swift` that its icon-only controls pair `.help()` with `.accessibilityLabel()`. Native `.help()` is the right tool in a native app, so the web rule against native `title` does not apply there. The audit doc now records this.\n\n## Approaches tried\n\nI first considered replacing every `title` with a per-component tooltip. I rejected that as out of scope and risky for a QA pass, since the styled tooltip already works and the real defect is the lost name. I also considered a headless Playwright pass to read computed accessible names after hover. The task allows headless checks only for visual verification, and a static guard plus the tooltip-code reasoning covered the same class more cheaply. I did not run a visual check.\n\n## Challenges and blockers\n\nThe first attempt at the guard used `fileURLToPath(new URL(..., import.meta.url))`. Under the UI vitest config `import.meta.url` is not a `file:` URL, so the suite failed at import. Using `__dirname`, as the sibling `mobile-touch-affordances.test.ts` does, fixed it.\n\nRunning `bunx vitest run` directly failed in unrelated suites with `localStorage` undefined. Using the repo's UI config (`--config src/ui-app/vite.config.ts`) fixed that, and the six affected suites passed (87 tests).\n\nThe scoped check's `tests` step was skipped because the remote runner executes the suite (`REPOOS_SKIP_TESTS=1`). I ran the directly affected suites myself, so the full-suite verdict is left to RepoOS.\n\nNo visual or screen-reader check was run. The change adds attributes and one wording change, so no layout or styling was expected to move. The audit states that a human screen-reader check of the four fixed controls has not been done.\n\n## Key learnings\n\nA title-only name is a latent accessibility bug whenever a helper rewrites `title` at runtime. The tooltip helper did its job for sighted users and quietly removed the only name for everyone else. Controls that rely on `title` for their name should also carry `aria-label`, and the guard now enforces that for icon-only controls.\n\nStatic template scans are cheap and deterministic, but heuristic. Quote-aware tag matching and fixtures for the edge cases matter more than the happy path: the round-1 false negatives came from a regex that looked right on ordinary markup. The guard also does not evaluate computed names, so a control built from a child component with its own name can slip past it. The audit says so.\n\n## Generated change summary\n\nBase tree: `b46c5e1fea21a56b65c4cce8fc2571cdb8030de1`\nSource tree: `07738e1f6eebdc4e555b071005edec6d90ce4258`\n\n- M docs/qa/2026-11-labels-tooltips-audit-qa.md: +7 / −1\n- M src/ui-app/tests/icon-control-names.test.ts: +29 / −2"
